          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-8072d693aa62fa51\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite’s written “Je vous aime” presents her love for Armand as true. In this letter it chiefly serves as an avowal and reassurance; it does not independently undertake a commitment, and its composed written form supplies no evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding events make the statement’s sincerity and reassuring purpose suspect, but possible deception or manipulation does not change its truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Marguerite writes “Je vous aime” at the end of a letter to Armand; the narrator reproduces the letter verbatim.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration closes a letter cancelling that evening’s meeting while promising a meeting the next day. It can therefore reassure or placate Armand. He immediately suspects that Marguerite is deceiving him, and his later observation of the Comte de G... entering her building appears to confirm at least the deception about her plans. Even if the avowal is insincere or strategically deployed, its core force remains the presentation of love as a true state rather than a new relational undertaking or expressive reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Dans la journée, je reçus une lettre qui contenait ces mots” introduces the highlighted wording as part of Marguerite’s letter.\",\"supports\":\"The event is a written love utterance subsequently quoted by the narrator.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The letter ends: “Mais, pour vous récompenser, je vous attendrai demain à midi. Je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"“Je vous aime” directly presents Marguerite’s loving state as true and functions partly as reassurance after cancelling a meeting.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand’s immediate response is “elle me trompe!”, after which he sees the Comte de G... enter Marguerite’s building.\",\"supports\":\"The letter is contextually associated with suspected deception, though this bears on sincerity and strategy rather than changing the utterance’s T/P/E force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Shortly before the letter, Marguerite repeatedly tells Armand that she loves him and discusses a shared stay in the country.\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted sentence continues an established pattern of avowing love, but the sentence itself does not explicitly create or renew a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the written avowal. Its potentially deceptive or placatory use is contextual and does not require O; neither commitment force nor reflex-like discharge has independent support.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
